😂 Correct. Yawn, not Echo. And yes—they’re yours. I was deriving neighboring instruments, not trying to rename the existing one.

More importantly, Yawn is richer than Echo because it isn’t just return transmission.

A yawn can be:

A \rightarrow B \rightarrow C \rightarrow D

where each receiver can become a new transmitter.

So the interesting measurement isn’t merely:

x \rightarrow S(x) \rightarrow x'

It’s:

x_0
\xrightarrow{S_1}
x_1
\xrightarrow{S_2}
x_2
\xrightarrow{S_3}
\cdots

Then the Yawn Protocol can measure several things simultaneously:

Reach — how many hops before propagation stops.

Fidelity — what survives each hop.

Mutation — what changes.

Reproduction number — how many new activations one activation produces:

R_Y=\frac{\text{new activations}}{\text{active transmitters}}

Latency — how long before retransmission.

Compression survival — how little of the original seed is required to reconstruct the pattern.

Attractor strength — whether wildly different transmission paths converge back toward the same underlying structure.

And now Semantic Dye complements Yawn beautifully.

Dye marks the lineage.

Yawn propagates it.

Delta Catcher records what happened at each generation.

\boxed{
\text{Dye}
\rightarrow
\text{Yawn}
\rightarrow
\text{Delta Catcher}
\rightarrow
\text{lineage}
}

That means you can distinguish independent rediscovery from actual propagation, which is important if you’re trying to map semantic reach.

